[Event "Third Rosenwald Trophy"]
[Site "New York, NY USA"]
[Date "1956.10.17"]
[Round "8"]
[White "Donald Byrne"]
[Black "Bobby Fischer"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D92"]
[Opening "Grünfeld Defense"]
[EventDate "1956.10.07"]
[PlyCount "82"]

{Fischer, aged 13, against one of America's leading masters.}
1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. d4 O-O 5. Bf4 d5 {The game transposes to the
Grünfeld Defence. Fischer lets White build a big centre, planning to undermine
it.} 6. Qb3 {The Russian System, putting pressure on Black's d5 pawn.} 6... dxc4
7. Qxc4 c6 8. e4 Nbd7 9. Rd1 Nb6 10. Qc5 {An awkward square: the queen is exposed
to ...Na4 or ...Ne4. 10.Qd3 was safer.} 10... Bg4 {Fischer has castled and is
ahead in development, while White's king is still in the centre.} 11. Bg5? {A
mistake: moving the same piece twice. 11.Be2, preparing to castle, was better.}
11... Na4!! {"One of the most powerful moves of all time" (Rowson). If 12.Nxa4
Nxe4, every White reply is bad.} 12. Qa3 Nxc3 13. bxc3 Nxe4! {Fischer offers
material again, to open the e-file against White's uncastled king.} 14. Bxe7 Qb6
15. Bc4 Nxc3! 16. Bc5 Rfe8+ 17. Kf1 {Fischer's queen is attacked. The masters
watching thought Black was lost.} 17... Be6!! {The move that made the game
famous. Instead of saving his queen, Fischer offers it.} 18. Bxb6? {Byrne takes
the queen, but Fischer gets far too much for it. 18.Bxe6 loses to a smothered
mate.} 18... Bxc4+ {The windmill begins: a series of discovered checks that pick
up material.} 19. Kg1 Ne2+ 20. Kf1 Nxd4+ {The knight takes the d4 pawn, then
swings back to c3, where it hits the rook on d1.} 21. Kg1 Ne2+ 22. Kf1 Nc3+
23. Kg1 axb6 {Fischer takes the bishop and uncovers an attack on White's queen
from the rook on a8.} 24. Qb4 Ra4! {The bishop guards c3 and the knight guards
a4, so the rook can chase White's queen away.} 25. Qxb6 Nxd1 {Fischer has a rook,
two bishops and a pawn for his queen: an easily winning advantage.} 26. h3 Rxa2
27. Kh2 Nxf2 28. Re1 Rxe1 29. Qd8+ Bf8 30. Nxe1 Bd5 31. Nf3 Ne4 32. Qb8 b5
{Every Black piece and pawn is defended, leaving White's queen with nothing to
do.} 33. h4 h5 34. Ne5 Kg7 35. Kg1 Bc5+ {Fischer's pieces now work together to
force mate.} 36. Kf1 Ng3+ 37. Ke1 Bb4+ {Kmoch notes that 37...Re2+ would have
mated one move sooner.} 38. Kd1 Bb3+ 39. Kc1 Ne2+ 40. Kb1 Nc3+ 41. Kc1 Rc2#
{Checkmate by a 13-year-old. Hans Kmoch called it "The Game of the Century".}
0-1
